            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book successfully unifies multiple divergent strands of the common interpretation—Christ's body, spiritual nourishment, and scriptural truth—into a single, coherent symbolic definition: the Word of God. By demonstrating that Jesus Himself is the Word made flesh, and that His 'flesh' in John 6 is 'the words that I speak unto you' (John 6:63), the book aligns the Christological reading with the explicit scriptural equations of bread and word (Deut 8:3, Matt 4:4). Furthermore, the book's definition effortlessly explains the secondary metaphors: if bread is the word/doctrine, then unleavened bread is pure doctrine (1 Cor 5:8) and leaven represents corrupting doctrine (Matt 16:12). The counter-interpretation fragments these into separate meanings, whereas the book uses Scripture's own explicit definitions to provide a unified rule.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Bread symbolizes the word of God.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Unleavened bread is pure doctrine\",\n    \"Leavened bread contains corrupting doctrine\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter applies the feeding of the widow and the fatherless directly to teaching the commandments, an application not printed in the glossary entry.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Bread primarily symbolizes Jesus Christ's body given in sacrifice (the Eucharist) and literal daily sustenance, with 'the word of God' serving as a distinct, secondary metaphor rather than the universal symbolic definition.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The physical/sacramental body of Christ, and material provision.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that all instances of symbolic bread represent the 'word of God', arguing that sacramental bread represents Christ's physical body distinct from His spoken or written doctrine, and that Deut 8:3 contrasts literal bread with the Word rather than equating them.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Deuteronomy 8:3 contrasts bread with the Word of God, implying that literal bread is not the Word but merely a physical counterpart.\",\n    \"The Last Supper narratives (Matthew 26:26) identify the bread explicitly as 'my body', which the common interpretation takes as His physical sacrifice.\",\n    \"1 Corinthians 10:17 identifies the many believers as 'one bread', which does not intuitively mean the church is 'one word of God'.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Provides a unified interpretation where the common view fragments the symbol into disconnected physical, sacramental, and doctrinal meanings.\",\n    \"Directly accounts for Jesus' explanation in John 6:63 that the 'flesh' (bread) He gives to eat is 'the words that I speak unto you'.\",\n    \"Perfectly explains the metaphor of leaven: if bread is doctrine, leaven is the corruption of doctrine (Matthew 16:12).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Provides a more intuitive reading of 1 Corinthians 10:17, where the church as 'one bread' maps easily to 'one body'.\",\n    \"Preserves the traditional sacramental distinction between the Word (Scripture) and the Table (Eucharist).\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Matthew 16:12\",\n    \"Matthew 4:4\",\n    \"Deuteronomy 8:3\",\n    \"1 Corinthians 5:7-8\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"The book does not explicitly address 1 Corinthians 10:17 to explain how the corporate body of believers constitutes 'one bread' if bread uniformly means the word of God.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
